; metacalc proof walk for the TI-84 Plus SE. pw_shape reads a theorem's stored proof straight from
; flash and counts the shape of the goal tree the host model builds from it (crates/mcui,
; crates/mcpack/src/mm/outline.rs): the steps, the distinct goals, the links from a goal to its
; subgoals and the nodes of the whole tree, a shared goal counted once for each use. It does not
; judge the proof: it compares no statement, substitution or disjoint pair, only the first symbol
; of the theorem's conclusion, of each essential hypothesis and of each referenced conclusion with
; the provable typecode.
;
; Code runs from flash in bank A and is position dependent. Assembling this file places nothing: an
; including file places it after the reader (lib/lib.z80), with -I at the repository root and at
; app/src. It is on the data page of the app (app/src/page_data.inc), where nothing calls it yet,
; and in the test image (crates/mcemu/tests/routines/reader_test.z80, with WITH_PW).
;
;   pw_shape      in:  HL = assertion number; B = the symbol id of the provable typecode, the
;                      library's `|-`; DE = a block of 8 bytes in bank B, clear of the state block
;                 out: A = status, 0 meaning ok, with the zero flag set exactly when A is 0; all
;                      registers but IX and IY are destroyed. On 0 the block holds, little endian:
;                        +0  2  steps: the numbers of the proof, a save marker not counted
;                        +2  1  distinct goals: the steps that make a goal and are not a reuse of
;                               a saved step
;                        +3  2  links: the goals under each distinct goal, added up
;                        +5  3  the nodes of the root's tree, a shared goal once for each use
;                      On any other status the block is not written.
;
; Method. lib_proof says where the theorem's record, reference list and proof lie. The walk maps the
; flash page that holds them in bank B through port 7 and reads them in place, and reads the head
; of the record of each assertion the references name from the page that holds it, with bounds
; checks of its own; it does not go through lib_read or the byte accessors. A stack of one byte an
; entry holds a goal's number, 0 to 254, or FFh for a syntax statement. A step number below the
; theorem's hypothesis count is a hypothesis: a goal when it is essential and its first symbol is
; the provable typecode, else a syntax entry. The numbers after those, up to the reference count,
; name the reference list: an entry of 8000h or more is a dummy variable, which takes nothing and
; gives a syntax entry; any other is an assertion, which takes as many entries as it has
; hypotheses and gives a goal when its conclusion's first symbol is the provable typecode, else a
; syntax entry. The numbers after the references name the saved steps, each a copy of an entry, and
; FFh saves the top entry. A goal's size is one more than the sizes of its subgoals, which are the
; goal entries among those its step takes, so the walk does not read how many of an assertion's
; hypotheses are essential. A number of F0h to FEh takes the next byte, as the kernel decodes it.
;
; The provable typecode is an argument and not the root's: a theorem whose conclusion starts with
; another typecode, and a proof whose one entry is a syntax statement, give PW_ERR_SYNTAX, the host
; model's "it proves a syntax statement".
;
; Memory. The tables and the walk's own stack are on the kernel's spare page, KERNEL_RAM_PAGE, in
; bank C through port 5, because the caller's stack is on a page port 5 takes away. The library is
; read in bank B through port 7, where the app's RAM is not. The walk borrows fm's statement store
; and fm's private stack and does not give them back: the statements fm had loaded are gone, and a
; caller that uses fm next loads them again with fm_assertion or fm_statement. It does not write
; the kernel's ready mark, fm's variables or open mark, FM_LOW, or any byte of either page but
; those named here and the bounce buffer.
;   PW_VARS   $E000, 59 bytes: the variables, at the start of FM_STACK
;   stack     down from FM_STACK_TOP, $E100: 8 bytes at most
;   FM_TOKENS $F100 +$000, up to 256 bytes: the stack of entries
;             +$100, up to 512 bytes: the saved entries
;             +$300, 3 bytes for each goal, up to 765: its size
;             +$600, 1 byte for each hypothesis of the theorem, up to 255: 1 for a goal
;             +$700, 2 bytes for each reference, up to 1,024: the assertion's hypothesis count, then
;                    1 when its conclusion starts with the provable typecode; both 0 for a dummy
;                    FM_TOKENS from +$B00 to its end is not written
;   the caller's block of 8 bytes, only on status 0, after the caller's port 7 is back
; and in the reader's state what lib_proof writes (its block, lr_dcbase and the scratch it lists)
; and, by pw_shape itself, lr_bufp, lr_k, lr_cn and, with the conclusion's first symbol, lr_hdr; the
; reads of lib_proof and of that symbol write the bounce buffer as every lr_read does.
;
; Ports and interrupts. Before the session the walk is the reader's: the caller's ports and
; interrupt state are back at every exit. The session reads the interrupt state with ld a,i twice,
; turns interrupts off, saves ports 7 and 5 in registers, maps the spare page, copies what it needs
; from the reader's state, stores SP in the spare page and switches to the private stack. From the
; port 5 write to that switch, and from the restore of SP to the port 5 write after it, no
; instruction touches the stack, which lies in the page being swapped. Interrupts are off for as
; long as port 5 or 7 differs from the caller's. Every exit of the session, whatever the status,
; puts port 7 back, writes the block when the status is 0, restores SP, then port 5, then turns
; interrupts on if they were on. IX, IY and port 20, the CPU speed, are never touched, and the
; shadow registers only by lr_read, after its own di.
;
; Bounds. lib_proof checks that the theorem's record, reference list and proof lie in one 16 KB
; chunk and inside the library, and the walk reads them from the one page that shows it. The head
; of another assertion's record is read with each pointer checked against the end of the page it
; lies in and the end of the library: a directory entry or a record the format forbids, or that
; leaves its chunk, gives 3.
;
; Stack. On the caller's stack the call goes at most 10 bytes below the return address: 2 for the
; call of lib_proof and its 8. On the private stack 8 bytes at most.
;
; Statuses, in the order found. The reader's: 4 before lib_open; 2 for a block not wholly in bank B
; or touching the state block; lib_proof's, 5 for a number at or past the assertion count, 3 for a
; record or proof it refuses, 9 for an axiom; 3 for a conclusion with no symbol or one that cannot be
; read. The walk's own are 32 and up, so that none is a reader's 1 to 10 or a kernel status:
;   PW_ERR_SYNTAX         42  the conclusion starts with another typecode than B
;   PW_ERR_REFS           44  more than 512 references
; then, as the reference list is read in order, before the first step: 3 for a referenced
; assertion's directory entry or record the format forbids or that leaves the library, and
;   PW_ERR_FORWARD        34  a reference to an assertion at or after the theorem, used or not
; then, in the order the proof meets them:
;   PW_ERR_CUT            41  a number of F0h to FEh with no byte after it
;   PW_ERR_BAD_REF        33  a number past the references and the saved steps made so far
;   PW_ERR_UNDERFLOW      32  a reference takes more entries than the stack holds
;   PW_ERR_SAVE_EMPTY     35  a save marker with an empty stack
;   PW_ERR_SAVED          40  a 513th save marker
;   PW_ERR_STACK          39  a 257th entry
;   PW_ERR_GOALS          38  a 256th distinct goal
;   PW_ERR_TOTAL          43  a goal whose size passes 16,777,215
; then at the end of the proof:
;   PW_ERR_NO_ENTRY       36  the stack is empty
;   PW_ERR_MANY_ENTRIES   37  more than one entry is left
;   PW_ERR_SYNTAX         42  the one entry is a syntax statement
; The caps on the entries and the saved steps are the kernel's CAP_STACK_ENTRIES and
; CAP_SAVED_ENTRIES (kernel/ram.inc).

#include "pw/ram.inc"
#include "pw/head.inc"
#include "pw/steps.inc"
#include "pw/enter.inc"
#include "pw/tables.inc"
#include "pw/find.inc"
